On Jun 7, 12:54 pm, Byron Arnold <byronarn...@gmail.com> wrote:
> Yesterday, I finally reached the Oracle.

Good.  Be careful now:  right about this part of the game, you may
start
running into some more difficult monsters.  However, you're also very
close to Sokoban.  If you can make it there, things get a little
easier
for a bit.

> Farthest I have ever made it. Of course, I bypassed the Gnomish Mines
> (I had read that the game is easier if you put off the mines until
> after Sokoban. Can anyone confirm/repusiate this for me?).

I have found that usually to be the case, yes.

It depends somewhat on your character's role, attributes, alignment,
and
equipment.  Going into the Mines right away as a Tourist, for example,
is basically suicide (unless you're *really* good at the game).  A
Samurai might be able to handle it.  Some people playing with the
Convict role patch have noted that Convicts can benefit significantly
from going into the Mines right away.

But typically, for a newish player, all else being equal, it's
generally
easiest to go for Sokoban first, IMO.

> It was no fun once I got there, though. While dipping my long sword in
> the fountains (I was a level 5 lawful knight), I called up a nymph who
> stole my (very rusty) sword.

Delphi is a very good place to dip for Excalibur.

I usually wait until after Sokoban.  By then my experience level is
usually over 7.  This is *arguable*, however.  The benefit of dipping
sooner is, once you get Excalibur, it's easier to survive.  Ultimately
I
think dipping as soon as possible versus a bit later is largely a
matter
of personal choice and playing style.

> Then there was another nymph, and a leprechaun. Betwee the three of
> them, they left me broke and defenseless.

Ignore the leprechaun for now.  You don't need money at this point in
the game.  You'd probably be saving it up until Minetown anyway, so
just
let the l have the gold until you have found Minetown, and then maybe
hunt him down on your next trip through the level, if it isn't too
much
trouble.  Or skip him and head downward: gold gets MUCH more plentiful
as you descend deeper into the dungeons.

Right now, the sword is a much higher priority than the zorkmids.
If you can get the sword back, take it and head for Sokoban.

Sokoban is an important goal in the early game.  You'll find food and
various other useful things.  Rings are sufficiently common in Sokoban
that there's a decent chance of finding one of the important ones
(e.g.,
regeneration or levitation).  Also you'll be able to identify the
scroll
of earth, and when you finish it you pick up either an amulet of
reflection or a bag of holding.  Plus, just surviving in Sokoban is
usually not too hard, compared to most of the rest of the game
(although
as always it pays to be a little careful, especially entering the
treasure zoo at the end), and you gain experience and come out a
little
stronger than before.

On several occasions I've been on the verge of death, with several
monsters chasing me that were too strong for me to fight, when I found
the entrance to Sokoban.  I went in there, healed, pushed some
boulders
around, picked up some useful stuff, and four levels later I came back
out and easily killed the things that had been killing me before.  It
can be an important turning point in the early game.

Sokoban is also a good place to go if you are, umm, "between" pets
(i.e., you've lost your starting pet for some reason).  Not only is it
easier to survive without a pet in there, but also, there's a good
chance of finding tripe and a halfway decent chance of running into a
domestic animal that you can tame.


